\\\\\\\"path\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"docs/design/yang.php.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"line_offset\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":180,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"n_lines\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":110}\\\\\\\"}}]}\\\\n{\\\\\\\"role\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"tool_call_id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"tool_MECjxTTBkFr3IE1110cVNMNl\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"content\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"180\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n181\\\\\\\\t- PHP has function scope only, with no block scope and no implicit closure\\\\\\\\n182\\\\\\\\t  capture, except arrow `fn`, which captures by value every enclosing variable\\\\\\\\n183\\\\\\\\t  it mentions, at creation.\\\\\\\\n184\\\\\\\\t- **Locals are cells** allocated at function entry with `:php/undef`; reading\\\\\\\\n185\\\\\\\\t  one emits `Warning: Undefined variable` and yields null. Every function body\\\\\\\\n186\\\\\\\\t  that declares locals or parameters releases them on exit (row 16).\\\\\\\\n187\\\\\\\\t- `global $x` binds the local to the `$GLOBALS` entry by reference (row 17),\\\\\\\\n188\\\\\\\\t  creating it as null if absent.\\\\\\\\n189\\\\\\\\t- `static $x = <expr>;` is a cell owned by the function object, allocated at\\\\\\\\n190\\\\\\\\t  function creation with an uninitialized sentinel. Its initializer is an\\\\\\\\n191\\\\\\\\t  arbitrary expression evaluated **when execution first reaches the\\\\\\\\n192\\\\\\\\t  declaration** (PHP 8.3) and filled once.\\\\\\\\n193\\\\\\\\t- **The dynamic-scope profile**: a function whose body contains\\\\\\\\n194\\\\\\\\t  `include`/`require`, `$$name`, `extract`, `compact`, `get_defined_vars`,\\\\\\\\n195\\\\\\\\t  `eval`, or one-argument `parse_str` keeps its locals in a heap symbol-table\\\\\\\\n196\\\\\\\\t  dict (the precedent is Python's module namespace, owner decision 1 of\\\\\\\\n197\\\\\\\\t  `yang.antlr.md` 8.5.1). Scope analysis decides per function, and the\\\\\\\\n198\\\\\\\\t  lowering targets `php/local-get`/`php/local-set` on the dict. All other\\\\\\\\n199\\\\\\\\t  functions use cells (owner decision B8.3). The symbol table holds reference\\\\\\\\n200\\\\\\\\t  markers like any location.\\\\\\\\n201\\\\\\\\t- **The global scope** is always the `$GLOBALS` dict: a heap dict,\\\\\\\\n202\\\\\\\\t  insertion-ordered, with the superglobals pre-seeded from the request record.\\\\\\\\n203\\\\\\\\t  `$GLOBALS` is read-only as of PHP 8.1 except through its elements (corner\\\\\\\\n204\\\\\\\\t  C13).\\\\\\\\n205\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n206\\\\\\\\t### 3.4 Superglobals and the request context\\\\\\\\n207\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n208\\\\\\\\t- A PHP run is one task consuming one **request record** from a\\\\\\\\n209\\\\\\\\t  composition-supplied stream: `{:php.request/method :uri :query :headers\\\\\\\\n210\\\\\\\\t  :cookies :body :server :env :files}`. `php/init!` builds `$_GET`, `$_POST`,\\\\\\\\n211\\\\\\\\t  `$_COOKIE`, `$_SERVER`, `$_ENV`, `$_FILES`, `$_REQUEST` (ordered per a\\\\\\\\n212\\\\\\\\t  pinned `request_order`) and, for CLI, `$argv` and `$argc`. `$_SESSION` is a\\\\\\\\n213\\\\\\\\t  bound surface (a session-store effect), absent by default. Nothing reads\\\\\\\\n214\\\\\\\\t  ambient process or HTTP state.\\\\\\\\n215\\\\\\\\t- **Output** (`echo`, `print`, inline HTML, `printf`) appends to an output\\\\\\\\n216\\\\\\\\t  cell with the output-buffering stack; `ob_start` and `ob_get_clean` are pure\\\\\\\\n217\\\\\\\\t  prelude over that stack. The top level flushes ordered `:php.response/body`\\\\\\\\n218\\\\\\\\t  records on the response stream. `header()`, `http_response_code` and\\\\\\\\n219\\\\\\\\t  `setcookie` produce `:php.response/header` records, refused after the first\\\\\\\\n220\\\\\\\\t  body flush with PHP's warning text.\\\\\\\\n221\\\\\\\\t- `exit` and `die` are an escape to the task root, followed by the shutdown\\\\\\\\n222\\\\\\\\t  sequence (row 22).\\\\\\\\n223\\\\\\\\t- Cross-request leakage is impossible by construction: one task per request\\\\\\\\n224\\\\\\\\t  and no store writes for guest state.\\\\\\\\n225\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n226\\\\\\\\t### 3.5 Coercion, comparison and arithmetic\\\\\\\\n227\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n228\\\\\\\\t- **Loose equality** is the PHP 8 comparison table, pinned as a fixture matrix\\\\\\\\n229\\\\\\\\t  over every pair of `null`, `true`, `false`, `0`, `1`, `-1`, `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"0\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`,\\\\\\\\n230\\\\\\\\t  `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"01\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"1e1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"10\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"abc\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"1 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`, `[]`, `[0]`, `0.0`,\\\\\\\\n231\\\\\\\\t  `NAN`, `INF`, an object, and a `Stringable`. For example `0 == \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`, `0 ==\\\\\\\\n232\\\\\\\\t  []` and `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" == []` are false in PHP 8, while `0 == \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"0\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" == null`, `[]\\\\\\\\n233\\\\\\\\t  == false`, `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" == \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"01\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"10\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" == \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"1e1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` and `100 == \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"1e2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` are true.\\\\\\\\n234\\\\\\\\t  Number versus numeric string compares numerically; number versus non-numeric\\\\\\\\n235\\\\\\\\t  string compares as strings, so `0 == \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` is false. Arrays compare by key\\\\\\\\n236\\\\\\\\t  and value; objects by property, or by identity for `===`.\\\\\\\\n237\\\\\\\\t- `<=>` uses the same ordering; `===` requires identical type and value,\\\\\\\\n238\\\\\\\\t  arrays element-wise, objects the same handle.\\\\\\\\n239\\\\\\\\t- **Numeric strings** (`is_numeric`) allow leading and trailing whitespace\\\\\\\\n240\\\\\\\\t  (8.0) and exponent forms. Leading-numeric strings in arithmetic warn (`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"5\\\\\\\\n241\\\\\\\\t  apples\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" + 1` warns and gives 6); non-numeric ones throw `TypeError`. String\\\\\\\\n242\\\\\\\\t  increment is supported (`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"++` is `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"b\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Az\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"++` is `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Ba\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`).\\\\\\\\n243\\\\\\\\t- **Integer overflow converts to float**: every integer operator goes through\\\\\\\\n244\\\\\\\\t  the exact integer module and demotes to float64 when the exact result leaves\\\\\\\\n245\\\\\\\\t  `[-2^63, 2^63-1]` (`PHP_INT_MAX + 1` is a float). This is the one place C3's\\\\\\\\n246\\\\\\\\t  promotion-before-overflow is used to detect rather than to promote.\\\\\\\\n247\\\\\\\\t- `/` yields an int only when exact, else a float. `%` is int-only:\\\\\\\\n248\\\\\\\\t  `PHP_INT_MIN % -1` is `0`, and `% 0` throws `DivisionByZeroError`.\\\\\\\\n249\\\\\\\\t  `intdiv(PHP_INT_MIN, -1)` throws `ArithmeticError`, and `intdiv(x, 0)`\\\\\\\\n250\\\\\\\\t  throws `DivisionByZeroError`. `fmod` never throws. `**` is an int when exact\\\\\\\\n251\\\\\\\\t  and in range, else a float, via the pinned `fpow`.\\\\\\\\n252\\\\\\\\t- **Float to string**: `echo` uses `precision=14` (`%.14G`, so `echo 0.1+0.2`\\\\\\\\n253\\\\\\\\t  prints `0.3`); `var_dump`, `var_export` and `json_encode` use\\\\\\\\n254\\\\\\\\t  `serialize_precision=-1` (the shortest round trip, `integer/float-digits`).\\\\\\\\n255\\\\\\\\t  Fourteen-significant-digit rounding needs exact decimal rounding of a\\\\\\\\n256\\\\\\\\t  binary64: a prelude function over `integer`'s exact decomposition, with no\\\\\\\\n257\\\\\\\\t  new kernel (an `integer` version bump only if measured too slow).\\\\\\\\n258\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n259\\\\\\\\t### 3.6 Null\\\\\\\\n260\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n261\\\\\\\\t`:php/null` is the only null. `??` and `?->` test it; `isset` is \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"bound and\\\\\\\\n262\\\\\\\\tnot null\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"; `empty` is falsiness; `unset` unbinds.\\\\\\\\n263\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n264\\\\\\\\t### 3.7 Classes, interfaces, traits and enums\\\\\\\\n265\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n266\\\\\\\\t- A class is a cell `{:php/type :class :name :parent :interfaces #{}\\\\\\\\n267\\\\\\\\t  :constants :static-props (cells) :props-decl :methods {name {:code\\\\\\\\n268\\\\\\\\t  :visibility :static? :abstract? :final? :declaring-class}} :abstract?\\\\\\\\n269\\\\\\\\t  :final? :readonly?}`. Instances are object cells (section 10.1).\\\\\\\\n270\\\\\\\\t- **Traits** are copied into the class descriptor at declaration time (class\\\\\\\\n271\\\\\\\\t  methods over trait methods over inherited ones; `insteadof`/`as` resolution;\\\\\\\\n272\\\\\\\\t  abstract and static trait members; trait properties merged with the\\\\\\\\n273\\\\\\\\t  compatibility check), which is PHP's own copy semantics and needs no runtime\\\\\\\\n274\\\\\\\\t  trait object.\\\\\\\\n275\\\\\\\\t- **Interfaces** are dispatch contracts: `instanceof` walks `:parent` and\\\\\\\\n276\\\\\\\\t  `:interfaces` transitively; interfaces carry constants.\\\\\\\\n277\\\\\\\\t- **Visibility** is checked at run time against the calling class context (a\\\\\\\\n278\\\\\\\\t  `:class-ctx` the lowering threads into methods), as PHP does; `protected`\\\\\\\\n279\\\\\\\\t  uses the inheritance relation.\\\\\\\\n280\\\\\\\\t- **Magic methods**: `__construct`; `__destruct` for acyclic garbage (section\\\\\\\\n281\\\\\\\\t  4, rows 48 to 51; cyclic garbage is corner C1); `__get`, `__set`, `__isset`,\\\\\\\\n282\\\\\\\\t  `__unset`; `__call`, `__callStatic`; `__toString` (`Stringable`);\\\\\\\\n283\\\\\\\\t  `__invoke`; `__clone` (row 52). `__serialize` is deferred (corner C18).\\\\\\\\n284\\\\\\\\t- **Declarations are enforced**: typed properties, parameter types, nullable\\\\\\\\n285\\\\\\\\t  and union types, and return types are checked at call or write time under\\\\\\\\n286\\\\\\\\t  the coercion rules, with `TypeError` under `declare(strict_types=1)` (a\\\\\\\\n287\\\\\\\\t  per-unit lowering option, pinned in the packet, default 0: coercive).\\\\\\\\n288\\\\\\\\t  Readonly properties follow rows 66 to 68.\\\\\\\\n289\\\\\\\\t- **Enums** (`enum X: string { case A = 'a'; }`) are singleton object cells\\\\\\\"}\\\\n{\\\\\\\"role\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"assistant\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"tool_calls\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"function\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"tool_i9H5chu9VNuMyS6Yd7eZe05d\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"function\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Read\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"arguments\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"path\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"docs/design/yang.php.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"line_offset\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":1600,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"n_lines\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":100}\\\\\\\"}}]}\\\\n{\\\\\\\"role\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"tool_call_id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"tool_i9H5chu9VNuMyS6Yd7eZe05d\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"content\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"1600\\\\\\\\t| Exceptions | `try`/`catch`/`finally`,       | handler stack with             |\\\\\\\\n1601\\\\\\\\t|            | multi-catch, `throw`           | `php/raise`; `finally` via     |\\\\\\\\n1602\\\\\\\\t|            | expressions, rethrow           | `php/try-finally`              |\\\\\\\\n1603\\\\\\\\t+------------+--------------------------------+--------------------------------+\\\\\\\\n1604\\\\\\\\t| Generators | `yield`, `yield k => v`,       | the C2 core under `php/gen-*`  |\\\\\\\\n1605\\\\\\\\t|            | `yield from`; `send`, `throw`, |                                |\\\\\\\\n1606\\\\\\\\t|            | `getReturn`                    |                                |\\\\\\\\n1607\\\\\\\\t+------------+--------------------------------+--------------------------------+\\\\\\\\n1608\\\\\\\\t| Modules    | `include`, `require`, `_once`; | section 11; ticks per section  |\\\\\\\\n1609\\\\\\\\t|            | `namespace`, `use`;            | 12                             |\\\\\\\\n1610\\\\\\\\t|            | `declare(strict_types)`,       |                                |\\\\\\\\n1611\\\\\\\\t|            | `declare(ticks)`               |                                |\\\\\\\\n1612\\\\\\\\t+------------+--------------------------------+-------------------------