pace/worktree-jing-cbor/collab/1790025400000-storage-engineer-jing-cbor-j0-fix.prompt.md-34-## Do (all of it, in order)\\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/collab/1790025400000-storage-engineer-jing-cbor-j0-fix.prompt.md-35-\\ncollab/1790025400000-storage-engineer-jing-cbor-j0-fix.prompt.md:36:1. qwen P2-1: in the generator's WITH_META handling, a metadata map that itself carries\\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/collab/1790025400000-storage-engineer-jing-cbor-j0-fix.prompt.md-37-   metadata is Refusal(\\\"malformed-frame\\\", ...) instead of an AssertionError. Add the\\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/collab/1790025400000-storage-engineer-jing-cbor-j0-fix.prompt.md-38-   decode-refusal fixture `meta/decode-meta-with-own-meta`. Record the ruling under\\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/collab/1790025400000-storage-engineer-jing-cbor-j0-fix.prompt.md-39-   A3/A10 in the README.\\ncollab/1790025400000-storage-engineer-jing-cbor-j0-fix.prompt.md-40-2. Self-tests: add `malformed-number` and `unsupported-value` to the required\"}],\"deferred\":false}}\ndbe9e072:collab/dao-jing/1790026900000-reviewer-jing-cbor-j1.qwen3.8-max.stdout.log:23823:{\"type\":\"event\",\"event\":{\"type\":\"run_end\",\"result\":{\"finalText\":\"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-21 22:40:00 GMT\\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-22 05:40:00 +07 (Indochina Time)\\nCoding-Agent: cmd\\nSession-ID: pending\\n\\n# Verdict: READY WITH CHANGES\\n\\nNo P1. The codec matches the frozen corpus semantics everywhere I could trace it (I hand-traced the reader/profile against every `mal/*`, `meta/decode-*`, `num/*` decode-refusal class and the numeric canonical boundaries). Two P2s, neither affecting frozen bytes or corpus acceptance, both small localized fixes; several P3 tightenings. Note: this session ran read-only with shell denied, so `git diff` could not be executed; the fixtures-helper claim was verified by file inspection (the J1 helper section is cleanly delimited, `cbor_fixtures.cljc:118-262`, J0 accessors above it untouched in shape) plus the orchestrator's status.\\n\\n## Findings\\n\\n**P2-1. No recursion-depth limit in the decode reader (unsafe-reader hardening, not a wrong-acceptance).**\\n`src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:911-991` (`read-item`) and `:1088-1139` (`item->value`) recurse per nesting level with no cap (same for `wire_of` `:812-829` on encode). An adversarial payload of ~10⁵ nested `81`-arrays or `d81b` frames overflows the host stack: JVM throws `StackOverflowError` — an `Error`, which the test's `refusal-of` (`cbor_test.cljc:70`, catches `Exception`) and any future ingress wrapper's `refusal` classification both miss — Node throws `RangeError`. Neither is an ex-info with `::refusal`. Plan sentence: decode sits at \\\"each ingress of bytes Jing did not encode itself\\\" (plan, *Layering*, lines 117-125), i.e. untrusted remote/DHT bytes by step 3/4; RFC 8949 §10 recommends nesting limits. Memory itself is input-bounded (every item consumes ≥1 byte; `length-of` `:877-885` refuses >2⁵³-1 heads as truncation; `need` checks before every copy), so this is stack-only. Smallest fix: thread a depth integer through `read-item`/`item->value` and refuse past a fixed cap (64-256; no corpus payload nests beyond ~4). The refusal class for it (frozen 16 have no depth class) is an architect choice — `:malformed-cbor` is defensible since no conforming producer emits such payloads. Must land before step 3; not a J1 byte blocker.\\n\\n**P2-2. Host-asymmetric decimal-exponent acceptance: identical canonical bytes accepted on Node, refused on JVM.**\\n`cbor.cljc:441-453`: JVM `decimal` refuses unless −exponent fits `[Integer/MIN_VALUE, Integer/MAX_VALUE]` (lines 446-449); the CLJS branch only requires `js/Number.isSafeInteger` (451-452). A canonical tag-4 payload with exponent ~10¹⁵ (< 2⁵³): Node decodes it, re-encodes identically, accepts; JVM refuses `:malformed-number` at `:1119`. That is the accepting host determining the boundary — against the plan's \\\"Do not let the accepting host determine this boundary\\\" (*Encoding contract*, line 163) — and it will trip J3's cross-host acceptance parity. The corpus pins no exponent window (A12 rules only bignum exponents out), so this is codec policy, not a corpus conflict. Smallest fix: mirror the JVM window exactly in the CLJS branch of `decimal` (refuse unless −exponent ∈ int32 range, same formula), and record the window for J2/Dart.\\n\\n**P3-1. Public carrier constructors bypass invariants; `num=` can disagree with `num-compare` for hand-built carriers.**\\n`cbor.cljc:319-365`: `(->Rational 2 4)` is publicly constructible. `num-compare` stays correct (cross-multiplication `:599-601` works on unreduced fractions), but `exact-key` `:556-563` keys on unreduced strings, so `num=` says false and `num-hash` differs for mathematically equal values — violating \\\"equal values compare zero and hash alike\\\" for that input. Decode and the `ratio` constructor (`:469-486`) always reduce, so nothing in the corpus or codec produces one; `Decimal.`/`Float64.` direct construction is benign (fields are the identity). Smallest fix: reduce in `exact`'s ratio arm (one `bgcd`), or make the deftype constructors private/validating.\\n\\n**P3-2. JVM `big` silently truncates non-integer Numbers.**\\n`cbor.cljc:101-109`: `(BigInteger/valueOf (long x))` — `(cbor/decimal 0 2.5)` or `(cbor/ratio 1.5 2)` computes garbage instead of refusing, against the plan's \\\"reject … loudly rather than computing garbage\\\" spirit (*Numeric identity*). CLJS `js/BigInt` throws natively. Fix: refuse non-integral `Number` input in the JVM arm of `big`.\\n\\n**P3-3. CLJS `num=`/`num-hash`/`num-compare` silently accept unsafe integral numbers.**\\n`cbor.cljc:380-381`: `host-integer?` admits any `Number.isInteger` number; `exact` then rounds it through `js/BigInt`, so `(num= 9007199254740992 9007199254740993)` is vacuously true on a value JS already rounded — while `encode` refuses the same input (`:745-747`, `:unsupported-value`). Plan: \\\"Reject unsafe integral JavaScript Number inputs\\\". Fix: in the CLJS arm of `exact` (not `host-integer?`, which feeds wire dispatch), throw \\\"not a portable number\\\" for integral numbers failing `isSafeInteger`.\\n\\n**P3-4. `exact-key` docstring overstates portability.**\\n`cbor.cljc:557-558` claims \\\"every host compares and hashes it the same way\\\". The key is host-independent, but its `hash` is not: JVM hashes strings with Murmur3, CLJS with the 31-based scheme, so `num-hash`/`equiv-hash` values differ cross-host for equal values. Fine for every J1/J3 requirement (hashes are only used within one host — `collapse?` grouping, host buckets), but if hash values ever cross hosts (published-index partitioning), a specified digest is needed. Fix the comment; record the limitation for the architect.\\n\\n**P3-5. Test tightening (mutation-thinking gaps).**\\n(a) `cbor_test.cljc:240`: the print-settings proof covers `(take 60 …)` of 209 canonical cases; the README obligation says \\\"the canonical cases\\\" — extend to all; cost is trivial.\\n(b) `cbor_test.cljc:50-57`: the `collapse-not-applicable` allow-sets encode the README's predicted rows, including rows the implementer's own finding 3 proved never fire (JVM: `set-int-ratio`, both NaN-payload cases; Node: `map-int-float`, `float-decimal`, `signed-zero`, `int-ratio`, `decimal-scale`). Harmless today (a skip requires an actual merge), but a future regression that starts merging — e.g. a broken `Float64 -equiv` merging ±0.0 carriers on Node — would silently skip instead of fail. Tighten to the observed merges: JVM `#{signed-zero decimal-scale vector-list integer-width}`, Node `#{vector-list set-nan map-nan}`.\\n(c) The decode-refusal runner (`:142-147`) is not `guarded`, and JVM `refusal-of` catches `Exception`, not `Throwable` — a P2-1 stack overflow would abort the whole deftest. Fold into the P2-1 fix.\\n\\n**P3-6. Housekeeping:** `node_modules` shows as untracked in git status despite `.gitignore:22` — an orchestrator tooling artifact; verify it is never committed. Not a J1 file.\\n\\n## Recommendation 3a — decoding without Boring: RATIFY\\n\\nThe Jing-owned reader is not a workaround; at Boring 0.1.30 it is the only implementation that can accept the frozen corpus. Decisive concrete case: Boring's decoder maps tag-30 `3/1` to `3N`, so canonical `num/ratio/3-1` (`d81e820301`) could never round-trip through a Boring decode — re-encoding `3N` yields `03`, and the corpus requires acceptance with rational kind retained (A11). The architect's own work package already declared the plan's \\\"read as `boring.data/UnknownRecord`\\\" sentence stale and prescribed closed dispatch; the implementer's probe results (stringref/date/UUID acceptance, with-meta mapping, `decode-seq` dropping a trailing index frame, JS float/int conflation) show the sentinel variant fails the same way, because Boring's lossy native mappings happen before any unknown-record callback. The design honors the plan's actual invariant — \\\"Validate tag/name/payload shapes before any lossy host conversion\\\" — literally: the generic reader holds only bytes/BigInt/text items and refuses `:float` items (`:1093`) without ever converting their bits; Boring remains the sole byte writer; the re-encode identity check (`:1154`) enforces shortest heads, ordering, reduced ratios, canonical NaN, and metadata omission by construction, all pinned by 132 decode-refusal + 209 canonical fixtures, so a Boring writer behavior change fails tests rather than silently altering bytes. Hidden risks of hand-parsing: stack depth (P2-1, fix before step 3) and per-item allocation overhead (each item is a map; ~constant×input amplification — bounded, but worth an ingress sizing note for remote/DHT). Conditions: ratify with the depth-limit fix, a formal plan correction of the decode-path sentence, and the allocation note.\\n\\n## Recommendation 3b — `:host-collapse` on Node: KEEP THE REFUSAL, RATIFY A NEW CLASS\\n\\nRefusing is unambiguously right: the two keywords are *not* equal under Jing's portable equality (`equiv` compares `[kind namespace name]` fields, `:605-607`), the bytes are canonical and distinct, and \\\"losing an entry is never acceptable\\\". No frozen class fits honestly — `:equality-collapse` would assert portable equality that does not exist; `:duplicate-key`, `:non-canonical`, `:malformed-cbor` are all false about these bytes. So: a 17th class, ratified by architect ruling as a README erratum (the frozen file stays unedited; the implementer correctly did not touch it). This does not contradict \\\"do not let the accepting host determine this boundary\\\": that sentence governs Jing's *acceptance rules*, which are host-independent here (raw-duplicate and portable-collapse checks run identically everywhere, and the decode-side twins `mal/collapse-*` enforce on every host); CLJS keyword interning (`-equiv` on the joined `fqn`) is a host data-model limit no acceptance rule can cure — Node cannot hold the value at all. Scope of the limit: CLJS **map keys and set elements** with colliding joined names only (`(keyword nil \\\"a/b\\\")`/`(keyword \\\"a\\\" \\\"b\\\")`, and analogously `(keyword \\\"a\\\" \\\"b/c\\\")`/`(keyword \\\"a/b\\\" \\\"c\\\")`/`(keyword nil \\\"a/b/c\\\")`); CLJS symbols are unaffected (`Symbol -equiv` is field-based), keyword *values* in vectors/lists are unaffected (no merging), JVM and — to verify in J2 — Dart are unaffected. Consequence to record for J3: `coll/map-both-slash-keywords` cannot round-trip on Node; the by-id skip plus the evidence test (`cbor_test.cljc:170-178`) is the right pin. If cross-host fidelity for this identifier class ever becomes a requirement, the fix is a CLJS keyword carrier in a future contract version — not a v1 change.\\n\\n## Checked and clean\\n\\n- **Ordering/duplicates before construction:** maps sorted by Boring under `:canonical` (pinned by `coll/map-mixed-keys`/`-reversed` sharing one hex, bytewise not length-first, incl. the `mal/map-keys-length-first` twin); sets self-sorted by lowercase-hex of encoded elements = unsigned bytewise, proper-prefix-first (`set-wire` `:781-784`); raw-byte duplicates and portable collapses refused before `(into {})`/`(into #{})` on encode (`members-wire` `:756-771`) and before construction on decode (`check-members` `:1013-1021`, `built` `:1024-1031`). Wire-node map keys can only host-merge when their bytes are equal, which the duplicate check refuses first — no silent entry loss.\\n- **UTF-8/surrogates:** strict decoders both hosts (`utf8` `:888-903`); encode-side surrogate scan over all text, identifier components, and metadata values under stripped keys (A9 validate-before-strip, `meta-wire` `:797-799`); decode-side `:invalid-utf8` fires during the structural read, before strip/canonicality — traced `meta/decode-invalid-utf8-under-line` (ED A0 80 under `:line`) exactly.\\n- **Identifiers:** two-arg `keyword`/`symbol` from fields on decode (`:1059-1062`), `namespace`/`name` on encode — no print/parse anywhere in codec paths (`pr-str` only in test messages; the JVM `print-method` is diagnostic); tag 39 refused unconditionally incl. nested (`id/tag39-map-key`, `-in-vector` traced).\\n- **Frames/metadata:** exact `[name payload]` via `pair-items`; closed five-name dispatch; all malformed arity/shape/name-bytes cases traced to `:malformed-frame`/`:unknown-frame-name`; A4 outermost with-meta; A5 strip scope (unqualified four keys, top level of every metadata map); P2-1/A3 bare-meta-map enforced on both sides (`:795-796`, `:1078-1079`); nested with-meta, meta-on-string/keyword/integer refusals traced.\\n- **Floats:** native float items carry only the ai, never converted, refused (`:1093`, `num/native-*` incl. half-float and in-vector); float32 widened exactly (`(double Float)` / `getFloat32`); every NaN → `7ff8000000000000` incl. signaling/negative/float32 NaNs; signed zeros and infinities raw-bit preserved; A13 decode of non-canonical NaN bits → `:non-canonical` via re-encode.\\n- **Numeric identity:** exact reduced-triple model — no double rounding anywhere (cross-multiplication compare, positive denominators guaranteed on every path incl. subnormals and ±0.0); native/carrier symmetry in both operand orders; −∞ < finite < +∞ < NaN with all NaNs equal; `Rational` carrier for denominator 1 on JVM (`:483-486`, since `clojure.lang.Ratio` cannot hold it); BigInt/long normalized by value; CLJS safe-integer/BigInt split. I attempted counterexamples for hash consistency and compare transitivity: none reachable through public constructors or decode — only via direct unreduced carrier construction (P3-1).\\n- **Boring adapter/effect proofs:** fixed `opts` pinned by test; stringref/shapes overrides throw under `:canonical` (pinned); ordinary one-item `boring/encode` only — the adapter never touches the decoder or indexed API; stringref absence pinned by hex check and by every frozen byte; trailing second item and index-like frame refused `:trailing-data`. A Boring byte-behavior change fails the corpus, not silently.\\n- **Tests:** refusal classes matched exactly per case; `guarded` runners; skips by id with printed reason; conditional N/A skips fire only on an actual host merge (otherwise hard failure) — tight; decode-side collapse twins run on all hosts, so encode-side host-merge N/As don't weaken the rule; equivalence/distinction groups re-verified against produced bytes; byte-isolation covers input, decoded, and payload mutation; print-independence covered (partially — P3-5a).\\n- **Implementer finding 3 (README N/A-table errors) — confirmed:** JVM holds `set-int-ratio` (Rational carrier is not `=` to Long) and both NaN-payload cases (Double hashing canonicalizes NaN bits → same bucket, `Numbers/equiv` false → held); Node holds `map-int-float`, `float-decimal`, `signed-zero` (carrier `-equiv` uses `Object.is`), `int-ratio`, `decimal-scale`, because the DSL builds carriers, not raw JS numbers. The README rows assumed raw host numbers; the table is wrong, the bytes are not. README correction is an architect action (frozen file).\\n- **Independence/safety:** no existing entry point edited (git status scope: only `test/dao/jing/cbor_fixtures.cljc` modified, helpers only); frozen resource never written (loader is read-only by construction); `:cljd nil` gating hides the entire body and the Boring require from the Dart compile; no ambient print dependence in any codec path; `dao.jing/sha256-bytes` exists (`src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:289`).\",\"stopReason\":\"end_turn\",\"turnCount\":20,\"usage\":{\"inputTokens\":2223878,\"outputTokens\":77094,\"cacheReadTokens\":2024064,\"cacheWriteTokens\":0},\"systemPromptTokens\":null,\"nextState\":{\"sessionId\":\"63497026-0c45-49ce-87ec-a8388422ce4d\",\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Created-GMT: 2026-09-21 21:50:00 GMT\\nCreated-Local: 2026-09-22 04:50:00 +07 (Indochina Time)\\nCoding-Agent: cmd (qwen/qwen3.8-max) | deepseek (shared brief; two independent reviewers)\\nSession-ID: pending (provider-generated) for cmd; caller-generated for deepseek\\n# Task: jing-cbor-j1: independent adversarial review of the JVM/Node canonical CBOR codec\\nRole: Adversarial Review (Storage & Encoding)\\nImplementers:\\n- Model: qwen/qwen3.8-max and deepseek-v4-pro | Assigned: 2026-09-22 04:50:00 +07 | Status: active | Rationale: the code was written by claude-opus-5 (Claude family); reviewers must differ; both families are owner-authorized\\n\\nRead-only. Work ONLY in /Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor (branch jing-cbor,\\nHEAD f5f71e95 = frozen J0 corpus; the J1 files are UNCOMMITTED in the working tree).\\nDo NOT edit or create any file and do not run tests (the orchestrator ran them: full\\nJVM green, focused 26 tests / 4269 assertions, CLJS 1601 tests, kondo and cljstyle\\nclean). You may read files and run read-only git commands (`git status`, `git diff`).\\nProduce the complete review now as your final response; do not wait for approval and\\ndo not promise a verdict.\\n\\n## What you are reviewing (new, untracked, plus one test helper edit)\\n\\n- src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc (about 1130 lines: codec, structural reader, portable\\n  numeric carriers and operations; the body is gated `:cljd nil`)\\n- src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor/boring.cljc (Boring 0.1.30 writer adapter)\\n- test/dao/jing/cbor_test.cljc (the corpus runner and numeric tests)\\n- test/dao/jing/cbor_fixtures.cljc (`git diff`: helper additions only)\\nSpec: docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md \\\"Encoding contract\\\" and \\\"Numeric identity\\\" IN FULL.\\nFrozen corpus (immutable, architect-ratified rulings A1-A17 and the refusal class\\nvocabulary in the README): test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.json and cbor-v1.README.md.\\nArchitect J1 completion criteria and Boring notes:\\ncollab/1790011562103-architect-jing-cbor-work-package.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md\\n(section J1, \\\"Effective Boring options\\\", and section 6 risks). The implementer's\\nreport is collab/1790026500000-ref-implementer-j1-report.md (read it; the two\\nitems in section 3 are quoted from it).\\n\\n## What to judge (concrete; cite file:line and corpus case ids)\\n\\n1. **Spec conformance.** Walk the codec against each J1 completion criterion: bytewise\\n   ordering and duplicate/collapse refusal BEFORE collection construction; strict UTF-8\\n   and recursive surrogate handling (validate-before-strip, ruling A9); identifier\\n   construction never via print/parse and tag 39 always refused; the four named frames\\n   are exactly `[name payload]` with CLOSED dispatch; the outer `clojure/with-meta` frame\\n   (A4) and the metadata rules (A5, P2-1); native CBOR floats refused; float32 widening;\\n   the single canonical NaN; decimal/ratio grammar. Is anything accepted that the\\n   plan rejects, or rejected that it accepts?\\n2. **Numeric identity.** `num=`, `num-hash`, `num-compare`, `equiv`, `equiv-hash`:\\n   exact comparison with no double rounding; native/carrier symmetry in BOTH operand\\n   orders; equal values compare zero AND hash alike across kind, scale and zero sign;\\n   -inf < finite < +inf < NaN with canonical NaNs equal; recursion through collections;\\n   the `Rational` carrier for denominator 1 on the JVM; BigInt vs long; the CLJS\\n   BigInt/number split (safe-integer numbers, BigInt). Try to construct a counterexample\\n   for hash consistency and for transitivity of compare.\\n3. **The two ratification items from the implementer (give your OWN recommendation\\n   for each; the architect decides afterwards):**\\n   a. **Decoding does not go through Boring.** The implementer probed Boring 0.1.30's\\n      decoder and found: tag 30 `3/1` decodes to `3N` (ratio kind lost); negative\\n      denominators accepted; stringrefs, tag 0/1 dates and tag 37 UUIDs decoded as\\n      ordinary values; it maps `clojure/with-meta` itself; `decode-seq` drops a trailing\\n      index frame; on JS a decoded native float is indistinguishable from an integer.\\n      So `decode` uses a Jing-owned structural reader (a port of the J0 Python checker),\\n      then re-encodes the decoded value through Boring and requires identical bytes.\\n      Boring stays the only byte WRITER. Is this sound? Is the Jing-owned reader a\\n      violation of the plan (which expects the codec to sit on Boring) or the correct\\n      reading of \\\"validate shapes before any lossy host conversion\\\"? Any hidden risk in\\n      hand-parsing CBOR here (recursion depth, allocation bombs, large lengths,\\n      indefinite lengths, huge tag/length heads)?\\n   b. **`coll/map-both-slash-keywords` cannot be decoded on Node.** ClojureScript keyword\\n      equality compares the joined name, so `(keyword nil \\\"a/b\\\")` and `(keyword \\\"a\\\" \\\"b\\\")`\\n      collapse to one map key. The decoder refuses this canonical case on Node with\\n      `:host-collapse`, a class OUTSIDE the frozen 16. Is that the right behavior, is a\\