 semantics. The test's collapse-not-applicable allow set is a superset (harmless entries never fire). One inconsistency check: the test's JVM allow set includes set-int-ratio-collapse, set-nan-payload-duplicate, map-nan-payload-duplicate (which per finding #3 are retained on JVM → never fire). Including these means: if a future JVM/Clojure version merges them, the test silently skips instead of failing. Minor looseness — P3: allow set entries that are known not to fire on a host weaken mutation detection; tighten to only observed merges (JVM: signed-zero, decimal-scale, vector-list, integer-width; Node: vector-list, nan×2). But note: allowing entries that the README predicted as N/A is defensible (README says implementer corrects the table). Actually — finding #3 says the README table is wrong for those rows; the allow set encodes the README's (wrong) predictions rather than observed behavior. The skip only fires on actual merge, so it's safe, but the mismatch with finding #3 is worth a P3: the report found the rows are wrong but the allow list retains them; the README correction (v1 errata) is an architect action.\\n\\nNow let me check the remaining corpus areas. Things to verify:\\n- meta cases (A9), surrogate cases\\n- num cases: bignum boundaries, ratios, decimals, float32 widening, NaN canonicalization\\n- decode-refusal classes match reader behavior: especially non-canonical vs malformed-cbor priority. Corpus says each decode-refusal case has exactly 1 defect so ordering can't matter — except for meta/decode-invalid-utf8-under-line. Let me spot-check some decode-refusal cases in the JSON against my reader trace. Let me grep the ids and refusals in the decode-refusal category.\\n\\nThe allow-set logic is fine. I'll survey the decode-refusal cases and metadata/surrogate cases in the corpus, then spot-check the reader against them.\\n\",\"signature\":\"\"},{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"call_db720c443ba5406fbc93c257\",\"name\":\"grep\",\"input\":{\"pattern\":\"\