anonical) to pin that validation precedes the canonical check.\\\"},\\n130:   {\\\"id\\\": \\\"uni/ascii\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"canonical\\\", \\\"categories\\\": [\\\"unicode\\\"], \\\"input\\\": {\\\"t\\\": \\\"str\\\", \\\"v\\\": \\\"hello\\\"}, \\\"hex\\\": \\\"6568656c6c6f\\\", \\\"sha256\\\": \\\"cb835593e4fa3633cc971fcbd1eb080ca322258220548eb7087beaf8ef6d7ff4\\\", \\\"refusal\\\": null, \\\"equivalence\\\": null, \\\"distinct\\\": [], \\\"plan\\\": \\\"dao.jing.cbor.md Encoding contract: UTF-8 without normalization; surrogate and invalid-UTF-8 rejection\\\", \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Plain ASCII text.\\\"},\"}]}],\"meta\":{\"source\":\"tool\",\"messageId\":\"1c874ac1-4d2f-4282-adea-8e6b1140114c\"}},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Let's verify the traces of some meta-decode cases against the code:\\n\\n- meta/decode-empty-meta: with-meta [{} [1]] → m = {} (map, no meta) → v = [1] vector, no meta → (with-meta [1] {}) — JVM: with-meta with {} → the meta is retained as {}. Then encode: meta-wire: m = {} — not nil → meta m is nil ✓ → doseq reader-position-keys contains? none → kept = {} → empty → w (no frame) → encode = 8101 ≠ input → :non-canonical ✓.\\n\\n- meta/decode-reader-meta: with-meta [{:line 1} [1]] → decode m {:line 1}, v [1] → with-meta → encode strips :line → kept is empty → no frame → non-canonical ✓.\\n\\n- meta/decode-meta-not-map: meta item is array [1] → item->value → vector → (map? m) false → malformed-frame ✓.\\n\\n- meta/decode-meta-on-string: v \\\"das\\\" → string → not vector/map/set/list/symbol → malformed-frame ✓.\\n\\n- meta/decode-meta-on-keyword: v is keyword → malformed-frame ✓.\\n\\n- meta/decode-nested-with-meta: outer with-meta, v = result of inner with-meta = [1] with meta {:b 1}… no, the inner is: with-meta [{:a 1} [1]]? Let's parse the hex: outer d81b82 71\\\"clojure/with-meta\\\" 82 a1(keyword :doc) 61\\\"das\\\"? Hmm, actually: `d81b82 71636c6f6a7572652f776974682d6d657461 82 a1 d81b8270...63646f63 6164 d81b8271636c6f6a7572652f776974682d6d65746182a1d81b827064616f2e6a696e672f6b6579776f726482f661620181 01`… The payload of the outer with-meta = [meta, value], where value is itself a with-meta frame over [{:b 1} [1]]. Decode the inner first? frame->value processes the outer: m-item, v-item; v = item->value(v-item) → inner with-meta → produces [1] with meta {:b 1} → outer check: v is a vector but (meta v) is not nil → malformed-frame ✓.\\n\\n- meta/decode-payload-three: with-meta payload array of 3 elements → pair-items nil → malformed-frame ✓.\\n\\n- meta/decode-flattened-form: tag 27 payload = 3-element array [name, meta, value] → item->value tag 27: pair-items p → nil (count 3) → destructuring binding of `(let [[n payload] (pair-items p)])` to nil → n nil, payload nil → `(when-not (= :text (:kind n))` → :kind of nil → nil ≠ :text → refuse malformed-frame \\\"tag 27 payload is not [name-string payload]\\\" ✓.\\n\\n- meta/decode-meta-with-own-meta: meta item = with-meta frame over a map → item->value produces a map with meta → (some? (meta m)) → malformed-frame ✓. On JVM does the map have meta? with-meta on a map — yes. ✓. On Node: with-meta on a CLJS map ✓ meta is retained.\\n\\n- meta/decode-invalid-utf8-under-line: hex `...a1 d81b8270...646c696e65 63 eda080 8101` — map {:line \\\"…\\\"} where the text 63 eda080 = 3 bytes ED A0 80 (encoded surrogate) → utf8 decode → during read-item for map item, refuses :invalid-utf8 ✓ — note: this happens during item->value of the with-meta meta map. But wait — the with-meta frame is only processed after item->value has decoded the inner items; the invalid text is deep inside the meta map → the :invalid-utf8 exception propagates before any other check ✓. Also, the :line key itself is non-canonical, but since utf8 refuses first, the class is correct ✓.\\n\\nHmm — but let me verify the order: read-item parses the entire byte sequence into a generic item tree, and utf8 decodes all text during reading (including text inside tag payloads) ✓. So invalid-utf8 fires at the reading stage, before trailing-data? Here there's no trailing data. Before malformed? No problem.\\n\\nOne order-of-operations concern: decode checks trailing-data after read-item but before item->value. If there's trailing data after a payload that contains invalid UTF-8, class = invalid-utf8 (reading stage) — the corpus says it's a single defect per case so no conflict.\\n\\nNext, encode-side surrogate cases 115-118:\\n- surrogate-under-stripped-line: vector [1] with meta {:line \\\"\\\\uD800\\\"} — no, `{\\\"utf16\\\": [\\\"d800\\\"]}` = a lone high surrogate string. meta-wire: doseq reader-position-keys: m contains :line → (wire-of \\\"\\\\uD800\\\") → check-text → refuse :unpaired-surrogate ✓ (A9).\\n\\nBut wait — the fixture's `input->value` constructs meta with `(with-meta coll (input->value m))`. On JVM, `(with-meta [1] {:line \\\"\\\\uD800\\\"})` is fine. And encode → meta-wire validates ✓.\\n\\n- surrogate-in-meta-key: meta keyword with name \\\"a\\\\uDC80\\\" (utf16 0061 dc80) → map-wire of the meta map → wire-of keyword → identifier-wire → check-text on name → refuse ✓.\\n\\nNext, num decode cases. Let's look at the num/* area — search for lines with ids starting with \\\"num/\\\". Also the frame/decode cases and id/decode cases (tag 39). Let's read around lines 131-323 in chunks, focusing on num and id decode refusals. Actually, let's grep for decode refusal ids with \\\"frame/\\\", \\\"id/\\\", \\\"num/\\\".\\n\",\"signature\":\"\"},{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"call_b77e9eeb8d604b7e9e60330a\",\"name\":\"grep\",\"input\":{\"pattern\":\"\\\"id\\\": \\\"(num|id|frame|bytes|host)/[^\\\"]*\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"decode-refusal\\\"\",\"path\":\"/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.json\",\"output_mode\":\"content\",\"head_limit\":60}}],\"meta\":{\"source\":\"model\",\"createdAt\":1790028171259,\"messageId\":\"bc1883bd-4e42-4ecd-a06e-4e3bd380856f\"}},{\"role\":\"user\",\"cont